		<h1>California NEM 3.0 Solar & Battery Payback Guide (2026 Rules)</h1>
		<p class="article-meta">Updated July 2026 • 6 min read</p>
		
		<p class="lead-paragraph">Under California's Net Billing Tariff (NEM 3.0), solar export compensation dropped by roughly 75%. Adding battery storage allows homeowners to capture daytime solar energy and avoid peak time-of-use (TOU) rates.</p>

		<h2>1. The Shift From NEM 2.0 to NEM 3.0</h2>
		<p>Under NEM 2.0, homeowners received near 1:1 retail credit for excess solar energy sent back to the grid. Under NEM 3.0, grid export values average approximately $0.05/kWh during peak solar hours, while evening grid electricity costs $0.50/kWh or higher across PG&E, SCE, and SDG&E territories.</p>

